N('fort-elizabeth-paulaula','Pāʻulaʻula (Russian Fort Elizabeth)','Waimea','hi','fort','ruins',21.951852,-159.664383,'site','public','walk','forts','1816–1817',
  'A stone star fort at the mouth of the Waimea River, the last Native Hawaiian fort left in the islands. Georg Anton Schäffer, a German doctor working for the Russian-American Company, talked the Kauaʻi chief Kaumualiʻi into a Russian "protectorate" and had Hawaiians build the fort to his plan; the Russian flag flew over it until Schäffer, found to have no backing from the Tsar, was made to leave in 1817. Kamehameha\'s side then held it as Fort Hipo. It is a National Historic Landmark, renamed from Russian Fort Elizabeth State Historical Park to Pāʻulaʻula State Historic Site.',
  'An irregular octagon of piled stone walls about 20 feet high, which Wikipedia says remain in good condition, with the foundations of the magazine, barracks and other buildings inside. A self-guided interpretive walk with a brochure.',
  'A Russian fort on Kauaʻi, built by Hawaiians for a German adventurer.',
  'Pāʻulaʻula State Historic Site, Hawaii Division of State Parks: daily during daylight hours, no entrance fee. But the same page carries a notice of March 21, 2025 that the park would close from March 24 for construction "until further notice", and it was still there in November 2026.'+STORM+' Check the page before going. Wikipedia puts the small parking lot south of the Route 50 (Kaumualiʻi Highway) bridge.',
  [['Hawaii State Parks: Pāʻulaʻula State Historic Site',DSP+'kauai/pa%CA%BBula%CA%BBula-state-historic-site/'],W('Russian Fort Elizabeth')],
  hours='Daily during daylight hours when open; closed for construction from March 24, 2025 until further notice',brick='no'),
N('kikiaola-menehune-ditch','Kīkīaola (Menehune Ditch)','Waimea','hi','water','traces',21.96973,-159.65663,'site','public','drive','','before 1778?',
  'A stone-lined irrigation ditch (ʻauwai) that Hawaiian tradition says the Menehune built. Hawaiians lined many taro ditches with stone, but very seldom with dressed stone: the 120 finely cut basalt blocks along about 200 feet of its outer wall led the archaeologist Wendell C. Bennett to call it "the acme of stone-faced ditches" (Wikipedia). On the National Register since 1984; the territory put up a marker in 1928.',
  'A row of squared basalt blocks along the inner side of Menehune Road, the remnant of one wall of the water-course (the 1928 marker\'s words); a small thing, worth a few minutes.',
  '',
  'Beside Menehune Road, a public road inland from Kaumualiʻi Highway in Waimea; the pin is the 1928 marker\'s position on HMdb. Look from the roadside and pull fully off the road; do not climb on the stones. The year is not known; the date given is only "before European contact" by tradition and is not confirmed.',
  [W('Kīkīaola'),['HMdb: Menehune Ditch marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=27646']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('grove-farm-museum','Grove Farm plantation homestead','Lihue','hi','landmark','standing',21.96632,-159.36819,'site','open','drive','','1864–',
  'One of the first sugar plantations in Hawaii, started in 1854 by Hermann Widemann and leased in 1864 to its manager George Norton Wilcox, whose family kept it for over a century. Wilcox brought water from the wet mountains to the dry lowland fields, an idea other planters copied. The homestead keeps the main house, renovated by C. W. Dickey in 1915, an 1877 cottage, an 1884 office, a hexagonal teahouse of 1898 and the Kaipu Camp workers\' houses of 1913 to 1917. It has been a museum since 1978.',
  'The furnished plantation house with its library and grand staircase, the outbuildings and the camp houses, seen only on a guided tour of about two hours. The page also mentions rides behind a restored 1925 steam locomotive, for which tickets are needed.',
  'A sugar family\'s homestead left as it was.',
  'Grove Farm Museum: visits only on guided tours, booked ahead (telephone (808) 245-3202); no tours on major holidays, and tours may be cancelled on rainy days. From Nawiliwili Road turn in at the entrance and take the immediate left over the old railroad tracks at the TO PARKING sign to the gravel lot (museum page, archived copy of February 3, 2026). Tour days and times are not on the copy read; a web search quoted Monday, Wednesday and Thursday at 10 a.m. and 1 p.m. from the museum\'s pages, not confirmed.',
  [['Grove Farm Museum: Visit (archived February 3, 2026)','https://grovefarm.org/visit'],W('Grove Farm (Līhuʻe, Hawaii)')],
  hours='Guided tours only, by reservation; not on major holidays (tour times not confirmed)',brick='?'),
N('kauai-museum-wilcox-building','Kauaʻi Museum (Albert Spencer Wilcox Building)','Lihue','hi','civic','standing',21.97504,-159.36828,'exact','open','drive','','1924–',
  'Kauaʻi\'s first public library building, paid for by Emma Kauikeolani Wilcox in memory of her husband Albert Spencer Wilcox, designed by Hart Wood and opened in 1924. A museum building named for William Hyde Rice went up beside it, and the museum opened on December 3, 1960; it later took over the library building too. On the State and National Registers since 1979.',
  'The 1924 Hart Wood building on Rice Street in the Līhuʻe civic centre, with the island\'s history inside; docent tours are offered. What original fittings survive inside is not checked.',
  '',
  'Kauaʻi Museum, 4428 Rice Street, Līhuʻe: no reservations needed (museum page). Admission charged; amounts not checked on the museum\'s own page.',
  [['Kauaʻi Museum','https://www.kauaimuseum.org/home'],W('Albert Spencer Wilcox Building')],
  hours='Monday to Friday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., Saturday 9 a.m. to 2 p.m., closed Sunday',brick='?'),
N('alekoko-menehune-fishpond','Alekoko (Menehune) Fishpond','Lihue','hi','water','ruins',21.94913,-159.37243,'site','view','drive','','1305–1420',
  'A Hawaiian fishpond made by walling off a bend of the Hulēʻia River near its mouth with an earthen wall about 2,700 feet long, faced with closely fitted stone on the river side. Tradition says the Menehune built it in one night; carbon dating in 2002 put the wall between 1305 and 1420. Neglected and overgrown with mangrove for much of the 20th century, it was bought by the Trust for Public Land in 2021 and passed to the nonprofit Mālama Hulēʻia, whose volunteers are clearing it and rebuilding the wall.',
  'The pond and its long wall seen from above, from the overlook the State of Hawaiʻi keeps on Hulemalu Road. The neighbouring Hulēʻia National Wildlife Refuge is closed to the public.',
  'A six-hundred-year-old fish farm said to be built in a night.',
  'The pond is private land of Mālama Hulēʻia (Wikipedia); see it from the state overlook on Hulemalu Road (US Fish and Wildlife Service page for Kīlauea Point, archived October 21, 2026). The pin is the middle of the pond; the overlook is on Hulemalu Road on the slope above it.',
  [W('Menehune Fishpond'),['US Fish and Wildlife Service: Kīlauea Point visit page (names the overlook)','https://www.fws.gov/refuge/kilauea-point/visit-us']],
  brick='no'),
N('waioli-mission-house','Waiʻoli Mission House and Mission Hall','Hanalei','hi','worship','standing',22.20094,-159.50192,'site','open','drive','','1834–',
  'The mission station at Hanalei Bay, founded in 1834 by the Rev. William Patterson Alexander and his wife Mary Ann, who were paddled from Waimea in the governor\'s double canoe. Alexander began the wood-framed house about 1835; the frame church was finished in 1841, and Wikipedia gives it as the oldest church building left on Kauaʻi. Abner and Lucy Wilcox followed, and three of their sons paid for the present Waiʻoli Huiʻia Church in 1912. Wilcox descendants had Hart Wood restore the house and the hall in 1921. National Register 1973.',
  'The mission house, now a small museum, which Wikipedia says shows Southern influence where most mission houses are New England; its rooms keep the family\'s things, among them an 1851 album quilt. The 1841 Mission Hall and the 1912 church stand beside it. Interior of the church not checked.',
  'A missionary home under the Hanalei cliffs.',
  'Waioli Mission House, run by Grove Farm, 5-5373 Kuhio Highway, Hanalei: tours first come, first served; no tours on most holidays, and tours may be cancelled on rainy days (Grove Farm page). The church is an active congregation; its service times are not checked.',
  [['Grove Farm: Waioli Mission House','https://grovefarm.org/waioli-mission-house/'],W('Waiʻoli Mission District')],
  hours='Tours Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday at 10 a.m. and 1 p.m.',brick='?'),
N('kilauea-point-lighthouse','Kīlauea Point Lighthouse','Kilauea','hi','light','standing',22.2317,-159.402,'exact','open','walk','','1913–',
  'A lighthouse on a narrow lava point on Kauaʻi\'s north shore, bought from the Kīlauea Sugar Plantation Company in 1909 for one dollar. With no road fit for the loads, materials came by sea and were hoisted up the cliff by a derrick; the light was dedicated on May 1, 1913. The 52-foot tower is reinforced concrete in Classical Revival style, and its second-order Fresnel lens is one of only seven left in an American lighthouse. Now named for Senator Daniel K. Inouye, inside Kīlauea Point National Wildlife Refuge.',
  'The white concrete tower with its walkway and lens, the oil house 155 feet away and the old radio beacon building that became a visitor center; the keepers\' three small stone houses stand about 1,000 feet south (Wikipedia). Seabirds nest all round the point.',
  'A lighthouse whose stones came up a cliff by derrick.',
  'Kīlauea Point National Wildlife Refuge, US Fish and Wildlife Service: reservations required for everyone 16 and older through Recreation.gov; adult entry with reservation fee $11 (page archived October 21, 2026). Arrive before 3:30 p.m.; guests are escorted out at 4 p.m.; closed on (or the Saturday before) major holidays. Parking is free; five minutes\' walk up a paved path to the point. No pets except service animals.',
  [['US Fish and Wildlife Service: Kīlauea Point, visit us','https://www.fws.gov/refuge/kilauea-point/visit-us'],W('Kīlauea Point Lighthouse')],
  th=[22.22927,-159.40227],thn='Refuge parking, Kīlauea Road',hours='Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., last entry 3:30 p.m.; reservation needed',brick='no'),
# ---------------- Maui ----------------
N('bailey-house-museum','Hale Hōʻikeʻike at the Bailey House','Wailuku','hi','landmark','standing',20.88578,-156.50711,'site','open','drive','','1833–',
  'A stone house of 1833, one of the first Western-style houses in Wailuku, at the mouth of ʻĪao Valley on the grounds of the royal residence of Kahekili II, last ruling chief of Maui. It became the home of the missionary teacher Edward Bailey. The Maui Historical Society opened it as a museum, Hale Hōʻikeʻike ("house of display"), on July 6, 1957. National Register.',
  'Exhibits on two floors of the old house and on the grounds, gardens of native plants, and a shelter holding Duke Kahanamoku\'s 1919 redwood surfboard (Wikipedia).',
  '',
  'Maui Historical Society, 2375A Main Street, Wailuku (museum page). Wikipedia gives longer hours, Monday to Saturday 10 to 4; the society\'s own page says Tuesday to Friday 10 to 2, given here. Admission not checked.',
  [['Maui Historical Society','https://mauimuseum.org/'],W('Bailey House Museum')],
  conf='mixed',hours='Tuesday to Friday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.',brick='no'),
N('kaahumanu-church-wailuku','Kaʻahumanu Church','Wailuku','hi','worship','standing',20.88611,-156.50525,'exact','view','drive','','1875–',
  'The fourth church on a site where Queen Kaʻahumanu heard the missionary Jonathan Smith Green preach in 1832; she asked that a church be named for her. A thatched church of 1834 and a stone church of 1840 came before this one, built in 1875 in plain New England Gothic under Edward Bailey, manager of the Wailuku Sugar Company. Its bell and the three clock faces of its Seth Thomas clock came round Cape Horn in 1884; the county named it the Town Clock in 1964. National Register 1975.',
  'The white church and its steeple clock at 103 South High Street; a stone structure with one opening stands behind it (Wikipedia). Hymns and prayers in its services are in Hawaiian. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An active church; seen from South High Street. Service times not checked: no page of the congregation\'s own could be found.',
  [W('Kaahumanu Church')],
  brick='?'),
N('halekii-pihana-heiau','Halekiʻi and Pihana heiau','Wailuku','hi','worship','ruins',20.90489,-156.49161,'site','public','walk','','1260–1790',
  'Two luakini (war and sacrificial) temples on a high ridge near the mouth of ʻĪao Stream, looking over the Nā Wai ʻEhā, the four watered valleys. Pihana began as a small temple between 1260 and 1400; Halekiʻi was added along the crest later, and both were enlarged to their present shape between 1662 and 1705. They were rededicated as war temples by Kahekili, and after Kamehameha\'s victory at Kepaniwai in 1790 his son Liholiho rededicated Pihana. National Register 1985.',
  'The remains of the two heiau on a ten-acre state monument (Wikipedia, state parks page).',
  'Two war temples above the four waters of Maui.',
  'Halekiʻi-Pihana Heiau State Monument, Hawaii Division of State Parks: daily during daylight hours. The pin is OpenStreetMap\'s point for the park; no parking area is named on the state page.'+KAPU,
  [['Hawaii State Parks: Halekiʻi-Pihana Heiau State Monument',DSP+'maui/halekii-pihana-heiau-state-monument/'],W('Halekiʻi-Pihana Heiau State Monument')],
  hours='Daily during daylight hours',brick='?'),
N('holy-ghost-church-kula','Holy Ghost Mission (Octagonal Church)','Kula','hi','worship','standing',20.76288,-156.32728,'exact','open','drive','','1894–1899',
  'An eight-sided Catholic church on the slopes of Haleakalā, perhaps the only historic octagonal building in Hawaii. Father James Beissel designed it and his Portuguese parishioners from the Azores and Madeira, who had come to work on the sugar plantations, built it; it was consecrated in 1899. It was closed for a year in 1991 for a major restoration. National Register.',
  'The white octagonal church at 4300 Lower Kula Road in Waiakoa. Inside detail not checked; the parish says the church is open to visitors on weekdays as given.',
  '',
  'Kula Catholic Community, 4300 Lower Kula Road: the parish page says the church is open to visitors Wednesday and Friday 11 a.m. to 3 p.m., and visitors are welcome at Mass; the parish gift shop is beside it.',
  [['Kula Catholic Community: Mass schedule','https://www.kulacatholiccommunity.org/massschedule'],W('Holy Ghost Catholic Church (Kula, Hawaii)')],
  hours='Open Wednesday and Friday 11 a.m. to 3 p.m.; Mass Saturday 5 p.m., Sunday 9:30 a.m., Thursday and Friday 7 a.m.',brick='?'),
N('piilanihale-heiau','Piʻilanihale Heiau (Kahanu Garden)','Hana','hi','worship','ruins',20.80388,-156.03988,'exact','open','walk','','1300s–',
  'Wikipedia gives it as the largest ancient place of worship in Polynesia: a platform of basalt blocks 341 by 415 feet, its front wall rising 50 feet in five stepped terraces on the side facing the sea. The main terrace dates to the 14th century. It stands in Kahanu Garden, a garden of the National Tropical Botanical Garden set up in 1972 on the Hāna coast, beside one of the last undisturbed hala forests.',
  'The stepped seaward wall and the great top platform with its smaller walled enclosures and pits, bounded at the back by a stone wall up to 8 feet high (Wikipedia); the garden\'s breadfruit collection round it.',
  'The largest temple in Polynesia, on the road to Hāna.',
  'Kahanu Garden, National Tropical Botanical Garden, 650 Ulaino Road, Hāna: self-guided and guided tours, booked online (guided tours may sell out); telephone (808) 248-8912. Days and hours are not on the garden\'s page read.'+KAPU,
  [['National Tropical Botanical Garden: Kahanu Garden','https://ntbg.org/kahanu'],W('Kahanu Garden')],
  th=[20.79902,-156.03739],thn='Kahanu Garden lot, Ulaino Road',hours='?',brick='no'),
N('makawao-union-church','Makawao Union Church','Paia','hi','worship','standing',20.892222,-156.350833,'site','open','drive','','1916–1917',
  'A stone church designed by C. W. Dickey, dedicated on September 2, 1917, on land near the old Paliuli sugar mill given by Henry Perrine Baldwin\'s family. The walls are reinforced concrete faced with native lava rock; four stained glass windows and the bell came from the older church. The 1938 earthquake damaged the community house beside it but not the church. National Register 1985.',
  'The lava-rock church on Baldwin Avenue with its four reused stained glass windows (Wikipedia); a Roosevelt pipe organ was bought for the earlier church in 1889. Whether it survives here is not checked; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Makawao Union Church, 1445 Baldwin Avenue: the congregation\'s page gives Sunday worship at 10:30 a.m.; telephone (808) 579-9261. The pin is the National Register point and was looked at on the aerial.',
  [['Makawao Union Church','https://makawaounionchurch.org/'],W('Makawao Union Church')],
  hours='Sunday worship 10:30 a.m.',brick='no'),
# ---------------- Molokaʻi and Lānaʻi ----------------
N('meyer-sugar-mill-molokai','R. W. Meyer Sugar Mill (Molokaʻi Museum)','Kualapuu','hi','industry','standing',21.160833,-157.008889,'site','open','drive','','1876–1889',
  'A small sugar mill on the upland Molokaʻi homestead of Rudolph Wilhelm Meyer, a German surveyor who settled at Kalae in 1853. Wikipedia says it ran from 1876 to 1889 on about 30 acres of cane, used 1850s methods and was worked by animals, not steam; the National Park Service calls it the 1878 mill and speaks of its operational steam engine. Restored as part of the Molokaʻi Museum and Cultural Center, it is the only 19th-century sugar mill in Hawaii with its processing machinery in place (Wikipedia). National Register 1979.',
  'The restored mill with its mule-driven cane crusher and steam engine (National Park Service), beside the museum.',
  'The one 19th-century sugar mill in Hawaii with its machines still in it.',
  'Molokaʻi Museum and Cultural Center, on Highway 470 (Kalae Highway) at the four-mile marker (National Park Service page for Kalaupapa). Hours not checked: no page of the museum\'s own was found.',
  [['National Park Service: attractions near Kalaupapa','https://www.nps.gov/kala/planyourvisit/nearbyattractions.htm'],W('Rudolph Wilhelm Meyer')],
  conf='mixed',hours='?',brick='?'),
N('kaunolu-village','Kaunolū Village Site','Lanai City','hi','town','ruins',20.734722,-156.964444,'area','view','hike','','before 1778–1880s',
  'A fishing village at the southern tip of Lānaʻi, abandoned in the 1880s and now the largest surviving ruin of an old Hawaiian village. Kamehameha I fished here and came here after conquering Maui, Molokaʻi and Lānaʻi; the Maui chief Kahekili visited too. A National Historic Landmark since 1962 and on the National Register since 1966.',
  'The remains of Halulu heiau, house sites, petroglyphs and Kahekili\'s Leap, a 60-foot cliff where warriors dove to show courage, with the islet Moku Noio in the bay (Go Hawaii). Bone fish lures were found in Ulaula Cave, a small lava tube nearby (Wikipedia).',
  'An abandoned royal fishing village at the end of a rocky road.',
  'Four-wheel drive only: Highway 440 west from Lānaʻi City toward Kaumālapaʻu Harbor, past the airport turnoff, left on Kaupili Road (unmarked dirt) about 2.5 miles to the heritage site sign and boulder, then right down a rocky road about 3 miles to the village (Go Hawaii page, archived September 17, 2025). Who owns the land was not checked. It is a sacred place: stay on the paths and do not move stones. The pin is the National Register point, in the general area.',
  [['Go Hawaii: Kaunolū Village (archived September 17, 2025)','https://www.gohawaii.com/islands/lanai/regions/south-lanai/kaunolu-village'],W('Kaunolū Village Site')],
  brick='no'),
